### How do you build complex sort orders?

The Comparator fluent API builds multi-field sort orders declaratively.

### How does comparing work?

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.Comparator;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class TradeSorter {

    // Single field: ascending by symbol
    Comparator<Trade> bySymbol = Comparator.comparing(Trade::getSymbol);
    // HDFC, INFY, RELIANCE, TCS, WIPRO

    // Descending: reversed()
    Comparator<Trade> bySymbolDesc = Comparator.comparing(Trade::getSymbol).reversed();
    // WIPRO, TCS, RELIANCE, INFY, HDFC
}
```

### How does thenComparing work?

```java
// Primary: symbol ascending. Secondary: amount descending.
Comparator<Trade> multiKey = Comparator.comparing(Trade::getSymbol)
    .thenComparing(Trade::getAmount, Comparator.reverseOrder());
// Within RELIANCE: 245050 before 123750 (descending)
```

### What is the reversed() pitfall?

```java
// WRONG: reversed() at end reverses ALL keys
Comparator<Trade> wrong = Comparator.comparing(Trade::getSymbol)
    .thenComparing(Trade::getAmount)
    .reversed();
// Symbol DESCENDING AND amount DESCENDING!

// CORRECT: reverse only amount
Comparator<Trade> right = Comparator.comparing(Trade::getSymbol)
    .thenComparing(Trade::getAmount, Comparator.reverseOrder());
// Symbol ASCENDING, amount DESCENDING within each symbol
```

### How do nullsFirst and nullsLast work?

```java
// Department might be null for some trades
Comparator<Trade> nullSafe = Comparator.comparing(Trade::getDepartment,
    Comparator.nullsLast(Comparator.naturalOrder()));
// Non-null departments sorted alphabetically, null departments at the END

Comparator<Trade> nullFirst = Comparator.comparing(Trade::getDepartment,
    Comparator.nullsFirst(Comparator.naturalOrder()));
// Null departments at the BEGINNING, then alphabetical
```

### How does comparingInt avoid boxing?

```java
// Boxing: comparing extracts Integer wrapper object
Comparator<Trade> boxing = Comparator.comparing(Trade::getQuantity);

// No boxing: comparingInt extracts primitive int
Comparator<Trade> noBoxing = Comparator.comparingInt(Trade::getQuantity);
```

### How does the fluent chain compare to manual code?

```java
// WRONG: manual compare (25 lines, error-prone)
class TradeComparator implements Comparator<Trade> {
    public int compare(Trade a, Trade b) {
        if (a.getDepartment() == null && b.getDepartment() == null) {
            // fall through to secondary
        } else if (a.getDepartment() == null) {
            return 1;  // null last
        } else if (b.getDepartment() == null) {
            return -1;
        } else {
            int deptCmp = a.getDepartment().compareTo(b.getDepartment());
            if (deptCmp != 0) return deptCmp;
        }
        int amtCmp = b.getAmount().compareTo(a.getAmount());
        if (amtCmp != 0) return amtCmp;
        return a.getTradeId().compareTo(b.getTradeId());
    }
}

// CORRECT: fluent chain (3 lines, readable)
Comparator<Trade> fluent = Comparator.comparing(Trade::getDepartment,
        Comparator.nullsLast(Comparator.naturalOrder()))
    .thenComparing(Trade::getAmount, Comparator.reverseOrder())
    .thenComparing(Trade::getTradeId);
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- thenComparing only evaluates when the previous comparator returns 0, making it efficient for high-cardinality primary keys.
- sorted() in streams is a stateful intermediate operation that buffers all elements before passing them downstream.
- For stable sorts (preserving order of equal elements), the fluent API is stable by default.
- Comparator.comparing with a Comparator argument is needed for nullsFirst/nullsLast and reverseOrder.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- Why use comparingInt instead of comparing? (Avoids autoboxing overhead for primitive fields.)
- Can you combine nullsFirst with reversed? (Yes: `comparing(field, nullsFirst(reverseOrder()))` puts nulls first, non-nulls in descending order.)
- Does sorted() preserve insertion order for equal elements? (Yes, it is a stable sort.)
